 agreed so i think  many developing countries particularly  the small island states left glasgow  feeling very very disappointed they also  asked for uh  for a  contributions to start being made from  the richer countries for loss and damage  that's the actual loss and damage they  experienced from climate change um that  was not agreed they just got some new  talks so really very little progress was  made in glasgow and of course since then  it's been it's too early to say uh  what's happening since then but there  are big expectations that more money  will be committed to this year and we  need to see some of that beginning to  emerge in the debates in developed  countries well yes and days i can see  you nodding there but what about the  year ahead because one of the the pluses  of cop 26 was this agreement to come  back at cop27 you know just in one year  rather than in five years with new  targets new commitments new pressure so  do you have some confidence that that  could benefit these smaller nations in  need of support  commitments are great but when they're  not followed through what do they mean  they mean nothing um you've already said  that it's been over a decade and we are  still yet to see the money that's needed  desperately by vulnerable countries all  across the world i think there needs to  be this recognition that as  a country who has contributed a large  amount to the exploitation of the  natural world but then also carbon  emissions we hold a responsibility to do  our best to support countries who are  going through literally the mess that we  have created and i don't think that  there is enough like i don't think  there's enough energy that's being put  into that at the moment well yes and  michael to go back to your point about  loss and damage as well because as you  say these countries that have  contributed the least to climate change  have already suffered many of them  suffered a lot of damage and they feel  that the owners should be on the richer  nations to pay for that um are they  getting anywhere on that you say no  progress at cop26 but is that argument  being heard out there do you think  um it's being uh it's being heard it's  in the world it's much more live now  than it used to be  because it's being experienced you can  see that loss and that and the damage  now in many  countries of which the small island  states are many of the most vulnerable  one of the most interesting ideas that  was put forward at  cop26 in glasgow was from the barbadian  prime minister that the global  currency that is issued  on occasion by the international  monetary fund the imf um so-called  special drawing rights or sdrs is a  little bit complicated but you can think  of it as a kind of global reserve  currency that money should specifically  be released by the international  monetary fund for climate uh development  in climate ah compatible development in  the worst affected countries including  for loss and damage  that idea which was raised as i say by  the barbadian prime minister  in a very powerful speech at the opening  session of cop 26  could get i think some legs this year um  it's unlikely i think to be done this  year but i think you could see many  developing countries getting behind that  proposal and because it's a way of  providing new money  it's something which uh i think many  developed countries in the end might say  yes this is something we should look at  okay and it would provide money  successively over several years and  that's the important thing okay an  interesting one to watch that one  michael we're going to move on to our  next topic though now and from launching  the earthshot prize to calling out  leaders at cop 26 the royal family is  often praised for speaking up on climate  change but critics say their support for  the green movement doesn't go far enough  they argue the monarchy has failed to  use its power and influence to encourage  real change at home and across the world  so is britain's most famous family doing  enough to tackle the crisis so days  prince charles has been campaigning on  the environment for decades hasn't he  prince william increasingly engaged and  we even heard the queen call for action  at cop26 so what's your take are they  doing enough  so my take is that this is extremely  convenient timing um to be talking about  the climate crisis when the royal family  is becoming more and more irrelevant in  public life um so i am highly critical  of the advances towards helping the  environmental movement especially given  that they are the biggest landowner in  the uk like one of the biggest  landowners in the uk and we are still  yet to see rewilding efforts on their  land and we're still yet to see land  being returned back to commoners from  when they originally were taken um these  are the kind of dramatic acts that we  need to reverse climate change in the  way that we're facing and i'm not seeing  that kind of like deliverables from the  royal family and um to talk specifically  about prince williams who has said some  very controversial things around  overpopulation rather than ignoring the  the like the actual problem is that we  consume too much and we have become too  um too used to living these really high  carbon lifestyles and what we need to do  is reduce this he doesn't talk about the  role that corporations are playing in  the destruction of our earth but instead  he focuses on  pollution population and population of  countries for example in the global  south who actually emit far less than  the average european country so i think  it's a little bit ridiculous quite  frankly okay that's interesting and i  know that prince charles has addressed  the issue of corporations getting on  board i think and michael um some would  say he's been campaigning for decades  his first speech on the environment was  in 1970 i think about 50 years ago  prince william's got his earth shot  prize handing out million pound prizes  so what what do you think do you agree  with days or do you think they are  making a tangible difference to people's  opinions  uh i don't know whether they're making a  tangible difference i'm sure there are  some people who are followers of  admirers of the royal family who may  receive the climate message from them in  uh in their reading or their  understanding of what prince charles and  prince william are saying and i suppose  if they're saying it and people some  people are listening to them then that's  a good thing it's raising awareness  amongst people who might not otherwise  get it they're not of course political  players their words don't really have  very much impact among governments  it's governments that speak to other  governments and not uh heads of state uh  who are merely ceremonial um i do think  it's uh the points that days makes are  legitimate if you are a public figure  you do need to be speaking on the things  that you have some control over and they  are  as she says landowners and so that's a  that's a really critical i think part of  their commitment if they are to be  making it but you know like other  celebrities  in the end if they speak about something  and it reaches some people that's good  um but it's uh governments and it's  public policies that really matter and  we shouldn't be distracted i think by uh  what they say when we're looking at the  politics we must leave it i can see days  nodding on that note of agreement we  will wrap things up and end the week on  on the pair of you nodding so daisy  gargie and michael jacobs thank you both  very much indeed for your time  so that's everything from us for today  but do you remember to listen to our  podcast climate cast and this week we  explore if germany is a climate leader  or a climate villain and that's  available now wherever you normally get  your podcast from thanks for watching  i'll see you next time  [Music]  you
 

